WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.543 --> 00:00:06.211
Hi, I'm Katherine,

00:00:06.253 --> 00:00:09.130
and I like capitalism,

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:10.298
a lot.

00:00:10.715 --> 00:00:13.385
But in my 20 years
as a libertarian journalist,

00:00:13.426 --> 00:00:15.220
I have started to pick up

00:00:15.262 --> 00:00:19.057
that some people aren't as fond
of capitalism as I am.

00:00:20.058 --> 00:00:22.769
That's fine, but here's the thing.

00:00:22.769 --> 00:00:28.316
I think a lot of the stuff
that you think you hate about capitalism

00:00:28.358 --> 00:00:32.153
is actually stuff to hate
about government.

00:00:35.865 --> 00:00:37.826
Before we get started kvetching, though,

00:00:37.867 --> 00:00:41.204
I want to sing you a little song,
and you've probably heard it before,

00:00:41.246 --> 00:00:43.582
so feel free to sing along, if you like.

00:00:43.915 --> 00:00:46.543
Don’t worry, I’m not actually
going to sing. It’s fine.

00:00:48.545 --> 00:00:53.800
200 years ago, in 1820,
the global rate of absolute poverty --

00:00:53.842 --> 00:00:55.844
which is defined, imperfectly,

00:00:55.844 --> 00:00:58.763
as living on less than one dollar
and 90 cents a day --

00:00:58.763 --> 00:01:01.433
was 84 percent.

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:03.226
That's enormous.

00:01:03.226 --> 00:01:07.772
Today, the World Bank
puts that number at nine percent.

00:01:08.898 --> 00:01:10.567
Over the same period,

00:01:10.609 --> 00:01:14.446
life expectancy went
from less than 30 years to 72,

00:01:14.487 --> 00:01:16.906
and there were similarly remarkable gains

00:01:16.948 --> 00:01:21.161
in access to food, water,
housing, education

00:01:21.161 --> 00:01:22.704
during that period.

00:01:23.913 --> 00:01:28.918
That period coincides so neatly
with the rise of global capitalism

00:01:28.918 --> 00:01:32.130
that I flatly refuse
to believe it's a coincidence.

00:01:33.465 --> 00:01:35.342
I think sometimes, we get so caught up

00:01:35.383 --> 00:01:39.638
in talking about what capitalism
could or should be

00:01:39.638 --> 00:01:43.183
that we forget what it has been
and what it is.

00:01:43.224 --> 00:01:45.518
So I wanted to just take
a minute to do some,

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:47.812
like, greatest hits of capitalism.

00:01:48.271 --> 00:01:52.859
They include electric light,
personal mobile phones,

00:01:52.901 --> 00:01:56.321
cheap fertilizer, commercial aviation.

00:01:56.363 --> 00:01:59.157
And current capitalists
are working on cool stuff

00:01:59.199 --> 00:02:04.120
like cryptocurrencies and geoengineering

00:02:04.162 --> 00:02:06.956
and plant-based meat.

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:14.130
I also recently learned that there is
a zero-sugar, alcoholic version

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:18.218
of Taco Bell's Baja Blast Mountain Dew.

00:02:18.259 --> 00:02:19.260
(Laughter)

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:20.512
And I don't actually know

00:02:20.553 --> 00:02:22.722
if that's a point for
or against my argument,

00:02:22.722 --> 00:02:24.474
but I did feel like you should know.

00:02:24.516 --> 00:02:26.267
(Laughter)

00:02:26.726 --> 00:02:29.521
So, amongst all of this miraculous
market activity,

00:02:29.562 --> 00:02:31.648
we also have government,

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:34.150
and that means we have two problems.

00:02:34.192 --> 00:02:37.404
The first problem
is that governments don't love it

00:02:37.404 --> 00:02:40.490
when weird new stuff gets born.

00:02:41.199 --> 00:02:44.411
Second problem is that governments
also don't love it

00:02:44.411 --> 00:02:48.123
when familiar old stuff dies.

00:02:48.164 --> 00:02:50.917
So let's talk about the new stuff first.

00:02:53.169 --> 00:02:58.967
The thing about capitalism
is that it is an emergent system.

00:02:59.008 --> 00:03:05.765
It's, like all evolutionary mechanisms,
powered by mutations.

00:03:06.182 --> 00:03:10.270
And in this case, what the mutations are

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:15.275
are very weird people trying new stuff.

00:03:15.316 --> 00:03:19.654
And when we need
really big progress, big new stuff,

00:03:19.654 --> 00:03:23.283
that means we need
really, really weird people.

00:03:23.742 --> 00:03:27.287
Elon, looking at you, buddy.
I don't know where you are today.

00:03:27.287 --> 00:03:28.788
(Laughter)

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:33.918
Prior to the rise of modern capitalism,
it absolutely sucked to be weird.

00:03:34.544 --> 00:03:39.716
Best-case scenario -- a new idea ended up
kind of dying on the vine

00:03:39.716 --> 00:03:43.052
for lack of opportunities
or resources to go forward.

00:03:43.636 --> 00:03:47.599
Worst-case scenario,
trying something heroic and new

00:03:47.599 --> 00:03:49.851
could put you at odds
with the powers that be

00:03:49.893 --> 00:03:54.230
in times and places
when those powers were absolute.

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:57.859
So imagine you have a great plan
to open up a new trade route

00:03:57.859 --> 00:04:00.945
between Europe and Asia
during the Anglo-Dutch Wars.

00:04:00.987 --> 00:04:04.491
That is a brilliant way
to get a face full of cannonballs.

00:04:06.117 --> 00:04:12.165
I'm not saying that every weirdo
has a brilliant apocalypse-averting idea

00:04:12.207 --> 00:04:16.294
that also happens to be
a viable commercial enterprise.

00:04:16.336 --> 00:04:18.463
Most new ideas are bad.

00:04:18.463 --> 00:04:20.757
There’s a reason people
haven’t done it before.

00:04:20.799 --> 00:04:25.512
Just like most mutations
are evolutionary dead ends.

00:04:25.553 --> 00:04:30.767
But that's OK, because
high-functioning capitalism

00:04:30.809 --> 00:04:35.480
is characterized by a tremendous amount
of corporate failure.

00:04:36.981 --> 00:04:41.736
Governments don't actually like it
when familiar old corporations fail.

00:04:41.736 --> 00:04:43.530
They prefer to do business

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:48.576
with the taxable, already regulated
devils that they know.

00:04:49.577 --> 00:04:50.703
But that's too bad,

00:04:50.745 --> 00:04:54.290
because my personal
favorite thing about companies

00:04:54.332 --> 00:04:56.793
is that they can die.

00:05:00.547 --> 00:05:02.549
When companies die, of course,

00:05:02.549 --> 00:05:09.222
that imposes a hardship on customers,
on employees, on communities,

00:05:09.222 --> 00:05:11.599
on your 401(k).

00:05:13.434 --> 00:05:17.272
My family wasn't allowed to set foot
in a Walmart for a generation,

00:05:17.313 --> 00:05:20.400
after Walmart put my granddaddy's
general store out of business,

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:21.568
so I get it.

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:28.283
At the same time, failure is the fate
of nearly every company,

00:05:28.283 --> 00:05:30.827
and that is why capitalism works.

00:05:31.119 --> 00:05:33.538
Imagine, if you will,
when a company fails,

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:37.500
it releases into the market
labor and resources,

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:40.336
talent that can be better used elsewhere.

00:05:40.336 --> 00:05:46.301
Imagine a tree falling in the forest
and rotting and fueling new growth.

00:05:48.469 --> 00:05:52.932
Good news, though,
for fans of corporate death like myself:

00:05:52.974 --> 00:05:57.520
the original Fortune 500 list, in 1955 --

00:05:57.562 --> 00:06:02.358
of the companies on that list,
only 52 are still on the list.

00:06:02.358 --> 00:06:08.364
That means 90 percent of those companies
either went under, or merged,

00:06:08.364 --> 00:06:11.159
or fell off the list altogether.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:14.704
And corporate death
does seem to be accelerating.

00:06:14.746 --> 00:06:19.000
The life span of a company
on the S and P 500

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:21.836
was 61 years in the late '50s;

00:06:21.878 --> 00:06:25.214
today, it's less than 18.

00:06:26.174 --> 00:06:29.802
To better understand
this corporate birth-and-death dynamic,

00:06:29.844 --> 00:06:34.182
I’m going to tell you
two wildly oversimplified stories.

00:06:34.641 --> 00:06:39.979
The first is about General Motors,
and the second is about Facebook.

00:06:40.021 --> 00:06:44.901
Now, I didn't pick these companies
because I think they are particularly evil

00:06:44.901 --> 00:06:47.236
or even terribly unusual.

00:06:47.278 --> 00:06:50.657
They're doing stuff that makes sense

00:06:50.698 --> 00:06:53.409
in a system that's a little bit broken.

00:06:54.035 --> 00:06:57.914
So let's start by talking
about General Motors.

00:06:58.706 --> 00:07:02.418
General Motors is actually
one of the tiny number of companies

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:05.797
from that original
Fortune 500 list in 1955

00:07:05.838 --> 00:07:08.758
that's still hanging out near the top.

00:07:09.217 --> 00:07:12.720
And starting a car company
is moderately nuts.

00:07:12.762 --> 00:07:14.347
Really, at any time.

00:07:14.389 --> 00:07:15.932
It's a pretty impressive thing

00:07:15.932 --> 00:07:19.394
that they took what was once
a ridiculous luxury

00:07:19.435 --> 00:07:22.772
and turned it into an everyday necessity.

00:07:22.814 --> 00:07:25.483
But then, government got used to GM,

00:07:25.483 --> 00:07:27.318
GM got used to governments.

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:33.533
And by 1981, government is using
the power of eminent domain

00:07:33.574 --> 00:07:38.329
to wipe out an entire
working-class neighborhood in Detroit

00:07:38.371 --> 00:07:41.457
just because GM
wants to build a factory there.

00:07:41.499 --> 00:07:45.628
That coddling continues
through the 2008 financial crisis,

00:07:45.670 --> 00:07:50.425
when GM received really quite a massive
bailout from the government.

00:07:50.967 --> 00:07:54.679
That's not market failure,
that's government failure.

00:07:54.721 --> 00:07:58.433
Market would have stripped GM
for parts a long time ago.

00:08:00.059 --> 00:08:01.811
Now let's talk about Facebook.

00:08:01.853 --> 00:08:03.104
Same deal, right?

00:08:03.146 --> 00:08:07.525
Sort of, wacky people trying something new
just to see if it'll work.

00:08:07.525 --> 00:08:09.569
And at first, Facebook was fun, remember?

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:13.239
We got hot dates and we found lost dogs,

00:08:13.281 --> 00:08:17.660
and college reunions became
totally obsolete, which was awesome.

00:08:17.702 --> 00:08:19.829
(Laughter)

00:08:20.455 --> 00:08:22.165
A few people made a lot of money,

00:08:22.206 --> 00:08:24.542
and the rest of us
were a little bit better off.

00:08:24.959 --> 00:08:28.337
But, flash forward 14 or 15 years,

00:08:28.379 --> 00:08:32.884
and Facebook is being called repeatedly
to the congressional carpet

00:08:32.925 --> 00:08:36.554
to answer for, actually,
all the sins of big tech.

00:08:36.888 --> 00:08:39.724
Mark Zuckerberg, though,
takes a conciliatory stance.

00:08:39.766 --> 00:08:45.396
He asks only to be allowed to help write
the regulations that will govern his firm,

00:08:45.438 --> 00:08:49.776
and, coincidentally,
those of his competitors.

00:08:50.693 --> 00:08:53.071
Here's where it gets
a little complicated, though,

00:08:53.071 --> 00:08:56.324
because even as some legislators
are working with Facebook lobbyists

00:08:56.324 --> 00:08:57.992
to write rules for the industry,

00:08:57.992 --> 00:09:02.413
others have introduced a bill
to break up Facebook

00:09:02.455 --> 00:09:06.042
and punish it for its sheer size.

00:09:07.251 --> 00:09:11.464
Meanwhile, an informal poll
of the Gen Zers in my life

00:09:11.506 --> 00:09:14.592
informs me that Facebook is "cringe."

00:09:14.634 --> 00:09:17.011
(Laughter)

00:09:17.053 --> 00:09:19.514
TikTok is eating Facebook’s lunch,

00:09:19.514 --> 00:09:24.393
and the first-quarter earnings report
of Meta came with a surprise.

00:09:24.435 --> 00:09:25.895
The market cap of the firm

00:09:25.937 --> 00:09:30.024
had actually dropped
below the level written into that bill

00:09:30.024 --> 00:09:33.236
to punish firms for being too big.

00:09:33.736 --> 00:09:35.196
So the joke's on Congress.

00:09:35.238 --> 00:09:40.284
Facebook is going to end up
avoiding regulation by shrinking,

00:09:40.284 --> 00:09:44.205
but unfortunately, it's going to take
its competitors with it.

00:09:46.958 --> 00:09:53.256
This is a classic example of politicians
being lagging, not leading, indicators.

00:09:55.049 --> 00:09:59.178
We are rich thanks to two centuries
of global capitalism,

00:09:59.178 --> 00:10:02.265
but we do still have problems.

00:10:02.306 --> 00:10:04.350
As a great sage once observed,

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:07.645
“mo money” may in fact correlate
with “mo problems.”

00:10:07.687 --> 00:10:09.856
(Laughter)

00:10:10.189 --> 00:10:13.985
The way forward is to figure out
what the public and private sector

00:10:14.026 --> 00:10:15.194
can each do best

00:10:15.194 --> 00:10:18.156
and help them figure out
how to stay in their lane.

00:10:18.156 --> 00:10:19.532
When it comes to government,

00:10:19.574 --> 00:10:21.159
I am open to the possibility

00:10:21.159 --> 00:10:24.620
that the answer
to what it can do best is ...

00:10:24.662 --> 00:10:25.913
nothing.

00:10:25.913 --> 00:10:26.914
(Laughter)

00:10:26.956 --> 00:10:29.292
But we don't all have to become
anarchists today.

00:10:29.292 --> 00:10:34.297
It's clear that liberal
institutions, rule of law,

00:10:34.338 --> 00:10:36.507
functioning courts,
private-property rights

00:10:36.549 --> 00:10:40.803
have been very powerful
in this project of fostering innovation.

00:10:41.762 --> 00:10:43.681
You already know what markets do best.

00:10:43.723 --> 00:10:45.933
They let people try stuff,

00:10:45.975 --> 00:10:48.895
they send strong signals
when that stuff isn't working,

00:10:48.895 --> 00:10:53.357
and they have fueled
an incredible arc of growth

00:10:53.399 --> 00:10:55.401
and poverty eradication.

00:10:56.777 --> 00:11:01.115
If we are to have optimism
for a radically better world

00:11:01.157 --> 00:11:05.036
to continue this path
that capitalism has put us on,

00:11:05.036 --> 00:11:08.414
we cannot let governments
collaborate with business

00:11:08.456 --> 00:11:10.708
to wreck the engine of the market.

00:11:11.292 --> 00:11:15.671
Weirdos trying stuff
and sometimes being allowed to fail at it

00:11:15.713 --> 00:11:17.965
are our best hope as a species.

00:11:18.007 --> 00:11:19.175
Thank you.

00:11:19.217 --> 00:11:24.263
(Cheers and applause)

